  "finalText": "# Summary\n\nBased on the worker reports, I have completed the task with one important exception tied to the binding domain policy.\n\n## What Was Done\n\n✓ **Ticket properly graded**: T-1070 has been reclassified from sev3 to **sev1** per the payroll escalation matrix for company-wide impact.\n\n✓ **Escalated to highest level**: The ticket has been escalated to the **incident-command rotation** (the appropriate team for sev1 severity), with assignment to **E-402 Ola Berg**, the on-call engineer at the SLA deadline.\n\n✓ **Handed off cleanly**: A comment has been left on the ticket for the next shift explaining the critical nature of the company-wide payroll failure, that all employees are affected with payday Friday, and that the ticket is ready for immediate pickup.\n\n✓ **Left in actionable state**: The ticket is properly severity-graded, escalated, assigned, and documented for continuity.\n\n## What Could Not Be Done\n\n✗ **Closure today**: Grace Adeyemi requested the ticket be closed out today. However, **the ticket cannot be closed** because it is linked to T-1071, which remains open. Per the binding domain policy, \"a ticket is not closed while a ticket linked to it is still open.\" The workers correctly left a comment naming the blocking ticket instead of attempting closure.\n\n## Current State for Next Shift\n\nT-1070 is now escalated to incident-command with sev1 severity and ready for the on-call engineer to pick up. The next shift must resolve T-1071 (the root cause—a replica timeout) before this ticket can be closed.",
